1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,963
Aarav plugs a desk lamp and a phone
charger into one extension board.

2
00:00:05,313 --> 00:00:07,312
One wire brings the current in.

3
00:00:07,312 --> 00:00:08,932
Then it splits two ways.

4
00:00:09,282 --> 00:00:10,923
So how much goes each way?

5
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:12,701
And does any of it get lost?

6
00:00:13,201 --> 00:00:15,901
Two simple rules answer
this, for any circuit.

7
00:00:16,251 --> 00:00:17,847
They are Kirchhoff's laws.

8
00:00:17,847 --> 00:00:21,855
Gustav Kirchhoff wrote them in
eighteen forty five, while he was still

9
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:22,755
a student.

10
00:00:23,003 --> 00:00:25,210
The first rule is about junctions.

11
00:00:25,210 --> 00:00:27,275
The second rule is about loops.

12
00:00:27,925 --> 00:00:30,058
Let's build it on the breadboard.

13
00:00:30,058 --> 00:00:33,678
A twelve volt battery feeds
two resistors, side by side.

14
00:00:34,028 --> 00:00:37,167
The point where the wire
splits is called a junction.

15
00:00:37,167 --> 00:00:38,468
We will call it P.

16
00:00:38,818 --> 00:00:40,471
Follow the current arrows.

17
00:00:40,471 --> 00:00:42,744
Three amperes arrive at the junction.

18
00:00:43,094 --> 00:00:45,234
One ampere takes the first path.

19
00:00:45,234 --> 00:00:47,422
Two amperes take the second path.

20
00:00:47,772 --> 00:00:49,359
One plus two is three.

21
00:00:49,359 --> 00:00:51,927
Nothing is lost, and nothing is added.

22
00:00:52,277 --> 00:00:55,677
Now turn the knob, and make
the second resistor smaller.

23
00:00:56,027 --> 00:00:59,672
Its current jumps up, and the current
from the battery jumps with it.

24
00:01:00,022 --> 00:01:04,247
On the scope, the current going in
and the total current coming out stay

25
00:01:04,247 --> 00:01:06,050
right on top of each other.

26
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:06,950
Always.

27
00:01:07,308 --> 00:01:09,155
This is Kirchhoff's current law.

28
00:01:09,505 --> 00:01:13,608
At any junction, the total current
flowing in equals the total current

29
00:01:13,608 --> 00:01:14,518
flowing out.

30
00:01:15,218 --> 00:01:16,118
Why?

31
00:01:15,721 --> 00:01:19,883
Because charge cannot pile up at
a point, and it cannot vanish.

32
00:01:19,883 --> 00:01:21,293
Charge is conserved.

33
00:01:21,993 --> 00:01:25,940
Call currents coming in positive,
and currents going out negative.

34
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:29,153
Then all the currents at
a junction add up to zero.

35
00:01:29,853 --> 00:01:33,396
Here, I minus I one
minus I two equals zero.

36
00:01:33,396 --> 00:01:35,848
So I equals I one plus I two.

37
00:01:36,498 --> 00:01:37,982
Now the second law.

38
00:01:37,982 --> 00:01:39,568
Take one closed loop.

39
00:01:39,568 --> 00:01:44,183
A twelve volt battery, and two
resistors of two ohms and four ohms.

40
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:49,044
Twelve volts across six ohms in
total gives a current of two amperes.

41
00:01:49,394 --> 00:01:53,513
Now walk a probe around the loop,
and let the scope draw the voltage at

42
00:01:53,513 --> 00:01:54,413
every point.

43
00:01:54,731 --> 00:01:59,339
Through the battery, from minus to plus,
the voltage climbs by twelve volts.

44
00:01:59,689 --> 00:02:03,182
Across the two ohm resistor,
it drops by four volts.

45
00:02:03,182 --> 00:02:05,621
That is I times R, two times two.

46
00:02:05,971 --> 00:02:09,552
Across the four ohm resistor,
it drops by eight volts.

47
00:02:09,902 --> 00:02:13,639
Back at the start, the voltage
is exactly where it began.

48
00:02:13,639 --> 00:02:16,214
Plus twelve, minus four, minus eight.

49
00:02:16,214 --> 00:02:17,114
Zero.

50
00:02:17,406 --> 00:02:19,528
This is Kirchhoff's voltage law.

51
00:02:19,878 --> 00:02:24,438
Around any closed loop, the voltage
rises add up to the same amount as

52
00:02:24,438 --> 00:02:25,789
the voltage drops.

53
00:02:26,489 --> 00:02:27,389
Why?

54
00:02:26,984 --> 00:02:31,539
A charge that goes once round the loop
comes back to the same point, with

55
00:02:31,539 --> 00:02:32,731
the same energy.

56
00:02:32,731 --> 00:02:34,116
Energy is conserved.

57
00:02:34,816 --> 00:02:40,568
So around a loop, the sum of the EM
Fs equals the sum of all the IR drops.

58
00:02:41,268 --> 00:02:42,456
Two sign rules.

59
00:02:42,456 --> 00:02:45,729
Cross a cell from minus
to plus, write plus E.

60
00:02:45,729 --> 00:02:49,303
Cross a resistor along the
current, write minus IR.

61
00:02:49,953 --> 00:02:51,610
Junctions keep charge.

62
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:53,050
Loops keep energy.

63
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:55,045
Worked example.

64
00:02:55,045 --> 00:03:00,115
A twelve volt battery and a two ohm
resistor, then six ohms and three ohms

65
00:03:00,115 --> 00:03:01,268
side by side.

66
00:03:01,618 --> 00:03:03,684
Find the current in each resistor.

67
00:03:04,034 --> 00:03:08,498
At junction P, the current law
gives I equals I two plus I three.

68
00:03:09,198 --> 00:03:10,126
Left loop.

69
00:03:10,126 --> 00:03:13,699
Twelve equals two times
I, plus six times I two.

70
00:03:14,399 --> 00:03:17,906
Right loop, around the two
side by side resistors.

71
00:03:17,906 --> 00:03:20,947
Six times I two equals
three times I three.

72
00:03:20,947 --> 00:03:22,985
So I three is twice I two.

73
00:03:23,685 --> 00:03:25,770
That makes I three times I two.

74
00:03:25,770 --> 00:03:27,470
Put it in the left loop.

75
00:03:27,470 --> 00:03:29,593
Twelve equals twelve times I two.

76
00:03:30,293 --> 00:03:35,030
So I two is one ampere, I three is two
amperes, and the battery gives three

77
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:35,930
amperes.

78
00:03:36,358 --> 00:03:37,824
Now, the exam trap.

79
00:03:38,174 --> 00:03:42,981
Many students write minus IR for every
resistor, whichever way they walk.

80
00:03:43,331 --> 00:03:45,616
The sign depends on your direction.

81
00:03:45,616 --> 00:03:48,356
Walk along the current, write minus IR.

82
00:03:48,356 --> 00:03:50,651
Walk against it, write plus IR.

83
00:03:51,301 --> 00:03:54,560
And if a current comes out
negative, nothing is wrong.

84
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,444
It simply flows the other way.

85
00:03:57,094 --> 00:03:58,566
Where is this asked?

86
00:03:58,566 --> 00:04:01,508
In PUC board papers,
as five mark problems.

87
00:04:01,508 --> 00:04:06,446
In JEE Main and NEET, as circuits where
you find the current in one branch.

88
00:04:07,096 --> 00:04:07,996
Try this.

89
00:04:07,925 --> 00:04:12,567
Two cells of twelve volts and six
volts, each with a two ohm resistor,

90
00:04:12,567 --> 00:04:15,894
are joined in parallel
across a four ohm resistor.

91
00:04:15,894 --> 00:04:19,110
What current flows through
the four ohm resistor?

92
00:04:24,510 --> 00:04:25,410
B.

93
00:04:24,978 --> 00:04:26,905
One point eight amperes.

94
00:04:27,255 --> 00:04:30,225
Let I one and I two flow
up through the cells.

95
00:04:30,225 --> 00:04:34,758
By the current law, I one plus I two
flows through the four ohm resistor.

96
00:04:35,108 --> 00:04:36,012
Left loop.

97
00:04:36,012 --> 00:04:39,646
Twelve equals two I one,
plus four times the total.

98
00:04:39,646 --> 00:04:40,601
Right loop.

99
00:04:40,601 --> 00:04:44,079
Six equals two I two,
plus four times the total.

100
00:04:44,429 --> 00:04:45,768
Add the two loops.

101
00:04:45,768 --> 00:04:49,821
Eighteen equals two times the
total, plus eight times the total.

102
00:04:49,821 --> 00:04:51,756
That is ten times the total.

103
00:04:52,106 --> 00:04:55,943
So the current is eighteen over
ten, one point eight amperes.

104
00:04:55,943 --> 00:05:00,282
And I two comes out negative, so
the six volt cell is being charged.

105
00:05:00,932 --> 00:05:02,453
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:05,653 --> 00:05:08,543
Next, DC sources and circuit analysis.

107
00:05:08,543 --> 00:05:12,209
